1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,393
Sweetheart, last week my granddaughter showed me

2
00:00:03,393 --> 00:00:05,939
a picture of a Christmas ornament in

3
00:00:05,939 --> 00:00:07,070
a fancy catalog.

4
00:00:07,250 --> 00:00:09,618
A little glass bird with a feather

5
00:00:09,618 --> 00:00:09,967
tail.

6
00:00:10,147 --> 00:00:11,528
Forty dollars.

7
00:00:11,708 --> 00:00:13,763
And I laughed so hard I had

8
00:00:13,763 --> 00:00:14,677
to sit down,

9
00:00:14,677 --> 00:00:16,884
because I have six of them in

10
00:00:16,884 --> 00:00:18,558
a shoebox in my attic.

11
00:00:18,738 --> 00:00:20,952
My mother bought them at the five

12
00:00:20,952 --> 00:00:23,367
and dime in nineteen fifty-one for a

13
00:00:23,367 --> 00:00:24,172
dime apiece.

14
00:00:24,521 --> 00:00:27,183
So tonight I'm giving you eleven vintage

15
00:00:27,183 --> 00:00:30,377
Christmas decorations that everybody threw away,

16
00:00:30,377 --> 00:00:32,706
and that the stores are now selling

17
00:00:32,706 --> 00:00:34,436
back to you for a fortune.

18
00:00:34,616 --> 00:00:35,614
For every one,

19
00:00:35,614 --> 00:00:37,823
I'll tell you how we used them,

20
00:00:37,823 --> 00:00:39,747
and how to get the look for

21
00:00:39,747 --> 00:00:40,816
almost nothing.

22
00:00:40,996 --> 00:00:42,253
And stay with me,

23
00:00:42,253 --> 00:00:44,913
because number eleven is the one I'd

24
00:00:44,913 --> 00:00:47,057
save if my house was on fire.

25
00:00:47,406 --> 00:00:48,428
Number one.

26
00:00:48,608 --> 00:00:50,837
The glass clip-on birds.

27
00:00:51,017 --> 00:00:53,924
Little glass birds with spun glass tails,

28
00:00:53,924 --> 00:00:56,618
clipped right onto the branches with a

29
00:00:56,618 --> 00:00:57,752
tiny metal clip,

30
00:00:57,752 --> 00:01:00,163
so they look like they just landed

31
00:01:00,163 --> 00:01:01,013
on the tree.

32
00:01:01,193 --> 00:01:03,805
Every house on Elm Street had a

33
00:01:03,805 --> 00:01:04,142
few.

34
00:01:04,322 --> 00:01:06,769
You can still find them at antique

35
00:01:06,769 --> 00:01:09,288
malls and estate sales for a couple

36
00:01:09,288 --> 00:01:11,807
of dollars, if you get there before

37
00:01:11,807 --> 00:01:13,390
the catalog people do.

38
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:15,017
Number two.

39
00:01:15,197 --> 00:01:16,450
Bubble lights.

40
00:01:16,630 --> 00:01:20,622
Tall little candle-shaped lights filled with colored

41
00:01:20,622 --> 00:01:23,078
liquid, and when they warmed up,

42
00:01:23,078 --> 00:01:24,537
the liquid bubbled.

43
00:01:24,717 --> 00:01:26,399
Danny and I lay on the rug

44
00:01:26,399 --> 00:01:28,599
under the tree and waited for each

45
00:01:28,599 --> 00:01:30,023
one to start bubbling.

46
00:01:30,203 --> 00:01:32,825
We made bets on which would go

47
00:01:32,825 --> 00:01:33,349
first.

48
00:01:33,529 --> 00:01:34,958
They still make them,

49
00:01:34,958 --> 00:01:36,796
honey, and ten of them on a

50
00:01:36,796 --> 00:01:39,110
tree make the whole room feel like

51
00:01:39,110 --> 00:01:40,472
nineteen fifty-five.

52
00:01:40,821 --> 00:01:42,075
Number three.

53
00:01:42,255 --> 00:01:45,421
Real tinsel, hung one strand at a

54
00:01:45,421 --> 00:01:45,901
time.

55
00:01:46,081 --> 00:01:47,856
Not thrown in clumps,

56
00:01:47,856 --> 00:01:48,786
sweetheart.

57
00:01:48,966 --> 00:01:51,315
My mother made us hang it one

58
00:01:51,315 --> 00:01:52,692
strand at a time,

59
00:01:52,692 --> 00:01:55,769
so it hung straight down like icicles.

60
00:01:55,949 --> 00:01:57,552
It took the whole evening.

61
00:01:57,732 --> 00:02:00,177
And on New Year's Day she took

62
00:02:00,177 --> 00:02:01,074
it all off,

63
00:02:01,074 --> 00:02:02,459
strand by strand,

64
00:02:02,459 --> 00:02:04,742
and laid it in a shoebox for

65
00:02:04,742 --> 00:02:05,557
next year.

66
00:02:05,737 --> 00:02:08,477
Nothing was thrown away in our house.

67
00:02:08,826 --> 00:02:09,953
Number four.

68
00:02:10,133 --> 00:02:11,810
The aluminum tree,

69
00:02:11,810 --> 00:02:13,767
with the color wheel.

70
00:02:13,947 --> 00:02:15,443
In nineteen sixty,

71
00:02:15,443 --> 00:02:18,187
my aunt in Cleveland got a silver

72
00:02:18,187 --> 00:02:20,848
aluminum tree, and a lamp on the

73
00:02:20,848 --> 00:02:23,924
floor with a wheel of colored plastic

74
00:02:23,924 --> 00:02:25,504
that turned slowly,

75
00:02:25,504 --> 00:02:27,250
so the tree went red,

76
00:02:27,250 --> 00:02:29,079
then blue, then green,

77
00:02:29,079 --> 00:02:29,910
then gold.

78
00:02:30,090 --> 00:02:32,771
My mother thought it was the ugliest

79
00:02:32,771 --> 00:02:34,409
thing she'd ever seen.

80
00:02:34,589 --> 00:02:36,896
Danny and I thought it was the

81
00:02:36,896 --> 00:02:37,434
future.

82
00:02:37,783 --> 00:02:39,165
Number five.

83
00:02:39,345 --> 00:02:40,239
Plaid.

84
00:02:40,419 --> 00:02:42,713
Red tartan ribbon on the wreath,

85
00:02:42,713 --> 00:02:45,294
a plaid blanket folded on the couch,

86
00:02:45,294 --> 00:02:47,373
a plaid bow on every present.

87
00:02:47,553 --> 00:02:50,037
Plaid is all over the magazines this

88
00:02:50,037 --> 00:02:51,968
year, and it was all over my

89
00:02:51,968 --> 00:02:54,589
mother's house in nineteen fifty-four.

90
00:02:54,769 --> 00:02:57,178
She bought one bolt of red plaid

91
00:02:57,178 --> 00:02:59,662
ribbon and used it for ten years,

92
00:02:59,662 --> 00:03:01,921
ironing it flat every January.

93
00:03:02,270 --> 00:03:05,596
To bring plaid back without spending much,

94
00:03:05,596 --> 00:03:08,447
honey, pick one plaid and repeat it.

95
00:03:08,627 --> 00:03:10,890
A plaid ribbon on the wreath,

96
00:03:10,890 --> 00:03:13,465
the same plaid on the stair rail,

97
00:03:13,465 --> 00:03:15,884
and a plaid scarf tied around a

98
00:03:15,884 --> 00:03:16,742
jar candle.

99
00:03:16,922 --> 00:03:18,969
Go to the thrift store for an

100
00:03:18,969 --> 00:03:21,227
old wool plaid skirt or blanket,

101
00:03:21,227 --> 00:03:22,568
cut it into strips,

102
00:03:22,568 --> 00:03:25,108
and you've got enough ribbon for the

103
00:03:25,108 --> 00:03:25,955
whole house.

104
00:03:26,135 --> 00:03:28,306
Three places, one plaid,

105
00:03:28,306 --> 00:03:30,930
and it looks like a magazine.

106
00:03:31,279 --> 00:03:32,510
Number six.

107
00:03:32,690 --> 00:03:35,302
Felt ornaments, made by hand.

108
00:03:35,482 --> 00:03:37,876
My mother cut little stockings,

109
00:03:37,876 --> 00:03:40,733
mittens and stars out of felt squares

110
00:03:40,733 --> 00:03:42,277
from the dime store,

111
00:03:42,277 --> 00:03:43,744
ten cents a square,

112
00:03:43,744 --> 00:03:46,446
and we sewed them with big stitches

113
00:03:46,446 --> 00:03:48,068
and glued on sequins.

114
00:03:48,248 --> 00:03:50,859
The children's hands made them crooked,

115
00:03:50,859 --> 00:03:52,868
and that was the beauty of it.

116
00:03:53,048 --> 00:03:54,656
Get a pack of felt and an

117
00:03:54,656 --> 00:03:56,972
evening, and you'll have a tree full

118
00:03:56,972 --> 00:03:59,995
of ornaments with your family's fingerprints on

119
00:03:59,995 --> 00:04:00,316
them.

120
00:04:00,666 --> 00:04:03,035
If this is bringing back memories,

121
00:04:03,035 --> 00:04:05,403
honey, tap the like button for me.

122
00:04:05,583 --> 00:04:08,218
It helps Grandma more than you know,

123
00:04:08,218 --> 00:04:10,413
and it tells me to keep going.

124
00:04:10,593 --> 00:04:12,513
In a minute I'll tell you the

125
00:04:12,513 --> 00:04:15,161
decoration my mother made every year out

126
00:04:15,161 --> 00:04:17,545
of things we'd normally throw in the

127
00:04:17,545 --> 00:04:17,942
trash.

128
00:04:18,292 --> 00:04:19,535
Number seven.

129
00:04:19,715 --> 00:04:22,431
Popcorn and cranberry garland.

130
00:04:22,611 --> 00:04:24,714
My mother popped a big pot of

131
00:04:24,714 --> 00:04:27,324
popcorn, left it out overnight so it

132
00:04:27,324 --> 00:04:28,701
got a little stale,

133
00:04:28,701 --> 00:04:31,818
because fresh popcorn breaks on the needle.

134
00:04:31,998 --> 00:04:34,200
Then we sat at the kitchen table

135
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:36,953
with needles and heavy thread and strung

136
00:04:36,953 --> 00:04:38,604
popcorn and cranberries.

137
00:04:38,784 --> 00:04:41,605
Three popcorns, one cranberry.

138
00:04:41,785 --> 00:04:44,479
Danny ate more than he strung.

139
00:04:44,828 --> 00:04:46,943
And when Christmas was over,

140
00:04:46,943 --> 00:04:49,512
we hung the garland outside on the

141
00:04:49,512 --> 00:04:51,098
bushes for the birds.

142
00:04:51,278 --> 00:04:53,155
The cardinals came first,

143
00:04:53,155 --> 00:04:54,656
then the chickadees.

144
00:04:54,836 --> 00:04:57,641
It's the only Christmas decoration I know

145
00:04:57,641 --> 00:04:59,283
that gets a second life.

146
00:04:59,463 --> 00:05:02,472
And here's the decoration made from trash

147
00:05:02,472 --> 00:05:03,573
I promised you.

148
00:05:03,753 --> 00:05:05,204
Paper chains.

149
00:05:05,384 --> 00:05:08,404
Strips cut from old wrapping paper,

150
00:05:08,404 --> 00:05:09,957
the Sunday comics,

151
00:05:09,957 --> 00:05:11,683
and Christmas cards,

152
00:05:11,683 --> 00:05:12,977
glued in loops.

153
00:05:13,327 --> 00:05:15,662
We started the paper chain on the

154
00:05:15,662 --> 00:05:18,562
first of December with twenty-four loops,

155
00:05:18,562 --> 00:05:21,037
and every night after supper one of

156
00:05:21,037 --> 00:05:22,523
us tore off one loop.

157
00:05:22,703 --> 00:05:24,424
When the chain was gone,

158
00:05:24,424 --> 00:05:25,930
it was Christmas Eve.

159
00:05:26,110 --> 00:05:28,631
It was our Advent calendar,

160
00:05:28,631 --> 00:05:30,499
and it cost nothing.

161
00:05:30,679 --> 00:05:33,941
My great-grandchildren make one every year now,

162
00:05:33,941 --> 00:05:36,231
and they fight over whose turn it

163
00:05:36,231 --> 00:05:36,995
is to tear.

164
00:05:37,344 --> 00:05:38,273
Number eight.

165
00:05:38,453 --> 00:05:40,450
Candles in every window.

166
00:05:40,630 --> 00:05:44,071
Electric candles, one in each front window,

167
00:05:44,071 --> 00:05:46,791
with the orange bulb shaped like a

168
00:05:46,791 --> 00:05:47,271
flame.

169
00:05:47,451 --> 00:05:48,533
In the fifties,

170
00:05:48,533 --> 00:05:50,915
you could walk down any street in

171
00:05:50,915 --> 00:05:53,512
Mansfield and see every house with a

172
00:05:53,512 --> 00:05:55,172
candle in every window.

173
00:05:55,352 --> 00:05:57,848
It was a way of saying welcome.

174
00:05:58,028 --> 00:06:01,035
Now people put up laser projectors.

175
00:06:01,215 --> 00:06:03,130
I'll take my candles.

176
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:05,732
And here's how to do the window

177
00:06:05,732 --> 00:06:07,184
candles cheap today.

178
00:06:07,364 --> 00:06:09,658
The battery candles with a timer,

179
00:06:09,658 --> 00:06:12,021
a few dollars each at the hardware

180
00:06:12,021 --> 00:06:12,438
store.

181
00:06:12,618 --> 00:06:14,393
Set them to come on at dusk

182
00:06:14,393 --> 00:06:15,773
and go off at eleven,

183
00:06:15,773 --> 00:06:17,877
and you never have to touch them

184
00:06:17,877 --> 00:06:18,272
again.

185
00:06:18,452 --> 00:06:21,129
Put one in every front window,

186
00:06:21,129 --> 00:06:22,736
upstairs and down.

187
00:06:22,916 --> 00:06:24,144
From the street,

188
00:06:24,144 --> 00:06:26,986
your house will look exactly like Elm

189
00:06:26,986 --> 00:06:29,290
Street in nineteen fifty-five.

190
00:06:29,639 --> 00:06:30,719
Number nine.

191
00:06:30,899 --> 00:06:33,082
The ceramic Christmas tree.

192
00:06:33,262 --> 00:06:36,147
A little green ceramic tree with tiny

193
00:06:36,147 --> 00:06:39,110
colored plastic bulbs that lit up from

194
00:06:39,110 --> 00:06:40,669
a light bulb inside.

195
00:06:40,849 --> 00:06:42,141
In the seventies,

196
00:06:42,141 --> 00:06:45,181
every grandmother in America made one in

197
00:06:45,181 --> 00:06:48,221
a ceramics class at the church basement.

198
00:06:48,401 --> 00:06:50,824
Mine is on my kitchen table right

199
00:06:50,824 --> 00:06:51,118
now.

200
00:06:51,298 --> 00:06:53,959
Today they sell for a hundred dollars

201
00:06:53,959 --> 00:06:56,116
and more at the antique shops.

202
00:06:56,296 --> 00:06:57,910
Check your mother's attic.

203
00:06:58,259 --> 00:07:00,263
So far, glass birds,

204
00:07:00,263 --> 00:07:02,466
bubble lights, tinsel,

205
00:07:02,466 --> 00:07:04,269
the aluminum tree,

206
00:07:04,269 --> 00:07:07,174
plaid, felt, popcorn garland,

207
00:07:07,174 --> 00:07:10,879
window candles, and the ceramic tree.

208
00:07:11,059 --> 00:07:12,220
Number ten.

209
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:14,618
Christmas cards on display.

210
00:07:14,798 --> 00:07:17,284
My mother strung a red ribbon across

211
00:07:17,284 --> 00:07:19,632
the doorway to the living room and

212
00:07:19,632 --> 00:07:21,980
hung every card we received with a

213
00:07:21,980 --> 00:07:22,739
clothespin.

214
00:07:22,919 --> 00:07:25,857
By Christmas, the doorway was covered.

215
00:07:26,037 --> 00:07:28,022
You could read the whole year of

216
00:07:28,022 --> 00:07:30,379
our family and friends on that ribbon.

217
00:07:30,728 --> 00:07:32,405
And now number eleven,

218
00:07:32,405 --> 00:07:34,539
the one I'd save if my house

219
00:07:34,539 --> 00:07:35,454
was on fire.

220
00:07:35,634 --> 00:07:37,224
The nativity set.

221
00:07:37,404 --> 00:07:40,250
Ours was cardboard and painted plaster,

222
00:07:40,250 --> 00:07:42,147
from nineteen forty-seven,

223
00:07:42,147 --> 00:07:44,774
with a wooden stable my father built

224
00:07:44,774 --> 00:07:46,379
out of an apple crate.

225
00:07:46,559 --> 00:07:49,099
Mary has a chipped nose from when

226
00:07:49,099 --> 00:07:52,178
Danny dropped her in nineteen fifty-two.

227
00:07:52,358 --> 00:07:54,804
The baby went in the manger only

228
00:07:54,804 --> 00:07:56,409
on Christmas morning,

229
00:07:56,409 --> 00:07:57,861
not one day before.

230
00:07:58,211 --> 00:08:00,769
My mother's rule was that the youngest

231
00:08:00,769 --> 00:08:02,922
child put the baby in the manger

232
00:08:02,922 --> 00:08:04,336
on Christmas morning,

233
00:08:04,336 --> 00:08:06,489
before anybody opened a present.

234
00:08:06,669 --> 00:08:09,032
Then the wise men moved a little

235
00:08:09,032 --> 00:08:11,617
closer every day until the sixth of

236
00:08:11,617 --> 00:08:12,207
January.

237
00:08:12,387 --> 00:08:13,839
I still do it.

238
00:08:14,019 --> 00:08:17,134
My great-granddaughter put the baby in the

239
00:08:17,134 --> 00:08:18,395
manger last year,

240
00:08:18,395 --> 00:08:19,730
and she whispered,

241
00:08:19,730 --> 00:08:20,694
welcome home.

242
00:08:20,874 --> 00:08:22,639
I'm still not over it.

243
00:08:22,988 --> 00:08:25,469
Now, how to get the vintage look

244
00:08:25,469 --> 00:08:27,795
without paying catalog prices.

245
00:08:27,975 --> 00:08:30,757
Go to estate sales in December,

246
00:08:30,757 --> 00:08:31,295
honey.

247
00:08:31,475 --> 00:08:34,958
Families clear out their grandparents' houses,

248
00:08:34,958 --> 00:08:37,608
and boxes of glass ornaments go for

249
00:08:37,608 --> 00:08:38,592
five dollars.

250
00:08:38,772 --> 00:08:41,419
Check the thrift stores the week after

251
00:08:41,419 --> 00:08:42,116
Christmas.

252
00:08:42,296 --> 00:08:44,200
And ask your own family.

253
00:08:44,380 --> 00:08:46,998
There's a box in somebody's attic or

254
00:08:46,998 --> 00:08:50,562
basement with your grandmother's ornaments in it,

255
00:08:50,562 --> 00:08:53,034
and nobody has opened it in twenty

256
00:08:53,034 --> 00:08:53,471
years.

257
00:08:53,820 --> 00:08:55,173
And mix them,

258
00:08:55,173 --> 00:08:56,421
don't match.

259
00:08:56,601 --> 00:08:59,074
The old trees were never perfect.

260
00:08:59,254 --> 00:09:01,178
A glass bird next to a felt

261
00:09:01,178 --> 00:09:03,317
mitten next to a ball with the

262
00:09:03,317 --> 00:09:04,386
paint worn off.

263
00:09:04,566 --> 00:09:06,986
That's what makes it look real,

264
00:09:06,986 --> 00:09:08,548
and warm, and yours.

265
00:09:08,728 --> 00:09:10,843
A tree that looks like it came

266
00:09:10,843 --> 00:09:12,606
out of a box looks cheap.

267
00:09:12,786 --> 00:09:14,806
A tree that looks like it came

268
00:09:14,806 --> 00:09:16,556
out of a life looks like a

269
00:09:16,556 --> 00:09:17,163
treasure.

270
00:09:17,512 --> 00:09:21,054
And handle the old glass ornaments gently.

271
00:09:21,234 --> 00:09:23,103
They're thin as an eggshell.

272
00:09:23,283 --> 00:09:24,895
Hang them by the cap,

273
00:09:24,895 --> 00:09:25,893
not the hook,

274
00:09:25,893 --> 00:09:27,352
and keep them high,

275
00:09:27,352 --> 00:09:29,425
out of reach of the cat and

276
00:09:29,425 --> 00:09:30,423
the toddlers.

277
00:09:30,603 --> 00:09:32,468
When you pack them away,

278
00:09:32,468 --> 00:09:34,800
wrap each one in tissue paper,

279
00:09:34,800 --> 00:09:37,209
and store them in an egg carton

280
00:09:37,209 --> 00:09:38,997
or a box with dividers.

281
00:09:39,177 --> 00:09:42,509
That's how mine have lasted seventy-five years.

282
00:09:42,858 --> 00:09:44,194
So one more time.

283
00:09:44,374 --> 00:09:46,931
Glass birds, bubble lights,

284
00:09:46,931 --> 00:09:50,056
real tinsel one strand at a time,

285
00:09:50,056 --> 00:09:51,761
